From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Scott Jordan <scott@spaceshipcreative.com>
Date: Thu, 8 Oct 2026 06:07:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 25/25] kv_cache: give the GLM-5.3-Flash sliding-window drafter
 its own block pool

With a DFlash2 drafter (5 sliding-window GQA layers, window 2048) on
GLM-5.3-Flash, the previous commit gives the drafter KV cache groups of
their own that draw block ids from the target's pool, so every
2304-token block carries a page per draft layer: 30% of the KV cache at
TP=4 and 46% at TP=2, though a sliding-window layer only ever holds its
window.

Give those groups a pool of their own:

- DraftSlidingWindowSpec (a SlidingWindowSpec, so SlidingWindowManager
  and its block recycling) marks the drafter's sliding-window layers
  when the pool is on. Like the kpool tail it opts out of prefix
  caching, which keeps its block size out of the hash block and
  cache_config.block_size. That block size is 256 when it divides the
  target block, else the target block; VLLM_GLM5NEXT_DRAFT_BLOCK_SIZE
  overrides it and must divide the target block.
- KVCacheConfig.draft_num_blocks is the pool's capacity, and
  draft_pool_group_ids, draft_layer_names, num_blocks_of_group and
  num_blocks_of say which ids and tensors index it.
  _glm5_next_draft_pool_blocks sizes it for max_num_seqs windows
  (window - 1 + retained + lookahead tokens, plus a block at each end),
  the max_in_flight_tokens in flight, one request's admission cap (so
  it does not refuse an admission the target's pool would grant) and
  the null block.
- get_kv_cache_config_from_groups takes the pool's bytes off the
  available memory before dividing the rest into target blocks, and
  lays the pool's tensors out after the target's. The target's offsets
  and strides are the same as without a drafter. Bytes per block, max
  concurrency, the peak-usage check, the num_gpu_blocks_override path
  and the cross-worker min-blocks reconciliation leave the pool's
  groups out of the per-block cost and add the pool once.
- KVCacheCoordinator builds a second, uncached BlockPool for those
  groups, and their managers allocate, recycle and free from it with
  caching and zeroing off. get_num_blocks_to_allocate checks the pool's
  free blocks. When the pool runs out it returns more blocks than the
  target pool holds, so allocate_slots fails and the scheduler waits or
  preempts, as it does when the target pool runs out.
- Model Runner V2 keeps the pool's ids out of copy-on-write (kv_caches),
  KV block zeroing, the warmup allocator (draft ids count and wrap in
  their own pool) and dummy profiling contexts (set_dummy_context takes
  a pool size per group). allocate_kv_cache already views each tensor
  with num_blocks_of.

Prefix caching: a hit covers the target only. The drafter's blocks
inside its window start fresh, so drafts read unwritten context and
acceptance can drop for up to one window (2048 tokens) after a hit. The
target verifies every draft, so the output does not change. Because the
drafter's group is both EAGLE-tagged and uncached, the target no longer
loses its last hit block to the drafter's EAGLE drop.

VLLM_GLM5NEXT_DRAFT_POOL (default 1) gates the pool. It also needs Model
Runner V2, which DFlash2 heads run on, and no KV connector, HiSparse or
context parallelism. If any of these is missing, or the gate is 0, the
layout is the previous commit's.

Source: the Kindling glm-5.3-flash-gx10 overlay,
experimental/fixes/{kv_cache_utils,kv_cache_coordinator,
kv_cache_interface,worker_utils,warmup}.py at commit c748079d. Its
experimental/README.md lists these as vLLM files with Kindling's
changes, keeping their Apache-2.0 headers; used with the owner's
permission. Kindling bind-mounts the files over the image's copies.
This commit reimplements the design natively against v0.31.0 and the
previous commit, with these differences:
- a spec type instead of env checks spread through the grouping and
  sizing code;
- the coordinator owns the second BlockPool;
- the pool's size includes in-flight, lookahead and admission terms;
- V2 copy-on-write and dummy contexts are handled;
- the gate covers runner, connector, HiSparse and context parallelism.
Kindling's VLLM_GLM5NEXT_DRAFT_POOL_BLOCKS size override is not carried
over.

Upstream context: vllm-project/vllm#55423 (@zixi-qi, a separate draft
region in the shared pool, closed in favour of #56983); #56983
(@Dovis01, DFlash for GLM-5.3-Flash, whose later revision books the
drafter inside the MLA group); and the generic packed layout of #55219.

Claimed gain (Kindling, DGX Spark TP=4, 26 GiB KV pin, 32 sequences,
block 2304): 1,416 -> 1,976 target blocks and 2.63M -> 3.67M KV tokens,
with a 105-block (0.58 GiB) pool. At max_num_seqs 32 and
max_num_batched_tokens 16384 this version's pool is 618 blocks of 256
tokens (0.38 GiB), which leaves 1,991 target blocks by the same
arithmetic. Not yet measured on GB10.

